{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Change 1717 ps2: LLM-Review +1 again, with 5 NEW advisories. Fixed all 5 — two were real\ncorrectness bugs in code I had just written, and one was a false claim in my own docs.\n\n1. (correctness) `_foldable_event_count` counted forward-compat UNKNOWN-TYPE events as\n   foldable, but the fold drops them: `_parse_candidate_events` skips any event whose\n   `event_type` is not in `KNOWN_EVENT_TYPES`, preserving a newer clone's event kind untouched\n   rather than squashing it. So selection promised work the fold would never do, and a ticket\n   whose excess was all unknown-type would be selected on every single sweep forever — the\n   exact churn class the earlier review round made me fix, reintroduced through a different\n   door. Selection now mirrors the fold's filter.\n\n2. (correctness) `_foldable_event_count` counted the ticket's OWN `-SNAPSHOT.json` toward the\n   foldable total: SNAPSHOT is a KNOWN_EVENT_TYPE and its filename ends in `.json`, so nothing\n   excluded it. Every settled ticket therefore reported one unit of outstanding work forever —\n   a permanent off-by-one that becomes a churn source at low thresholds. A snapshot is\n   already-folded STATE, not pending work; it is excluded now. Both fixes have regression tests.\n\n3. (docs) I claimed in `docs/concurrency.md` and in the workflow header that the sweep runs in\n   a clone with \"its own `.git`, its own index\". That is wrong: the workflow mounts the store\n   with `git worktree add`, and a worktree SHARES the checkout's object store. The isolation\n   that actually matters is that the runner is a separate checkout on a separate machine, so\n   it shares no `index.lock`, no `rebar-git-op.lock` and no store write lock with an\n   interactive session. Corrected in both places rather than left as a plausible-sounding\n   overstatement.\n\n4. (iac) The sweep had no failure notification or telemetry, unlike the reconcile-bridge\n   template it copies. Compaction is invisible when it works and equally invisible when it\n   silently stops, so a store whose sweep has quietly folded nothing for weeks looks healthy.\n   Added a step-summary report of the sweep's counts on every run and a failure notification\n   mirroring the bridge's. Its text also states the honest severity: events stay live and\n   valid, and a CI-less store still compacts via the operation-linked trigger, so a failed\n   sweep degrades cadence, not correctness.\n\n5. (tests) The exit-2 path was untested even though the workflow BRANCHES on it (warn and\n   pass) versus any other non-zero (fail). Added tests that a per-ticket fold failure yields\n   exit 2, that it does not stop the sweep folding the other tickets, and that a clean sweep is\n   exit 0.\n\nAlso fixed while wiring the operation-linked trigger: adding its call to `close_ticket` pushed\nthat function from 17 to 18 complexity, and `.github/complexity-baseline.json` is a shrink-only\nratchet. Rather than raise the ceiling I extracted `_trigger_compaction` as a guard function so\nthe call site stays unconditional — the same pattern, and the same stated reason, as\n`_ensure_duplicate_close_is_linked` in `close_precheck`.\n\nps2's Verified -1 was the SAME known order-dependent reconciler test on the same macOS shard\n(`test_pass_summary_reports_the_decline_count`), already triaged: introduced by 4010da6493\nyesterday, fix in flight, not reachable from this diff. I did not recheck it for the same\nreason as last time."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786614580141354001,"uuid":"53bb088a-c17f-461a-af21-fa5359b7af1a"}